  "welcomeTitle": "Welcome",
  "introDialogBody": "Loneliness, or the inability to connect with others, is a feeling, not a disease. So we will help you as a human journey rather than a pill you take every day. This app isn't a magic tool for not being lonely, but rather a helper in overcoming this feeling. Since you have downloaded this app, I think I can start this journey with you. We will start slowly with small talk, like asking someone what time it is. If you don't want to do a task, press \"Another Task\" and the app will randomly pick another one for you. This option is unlimited, but remember time isn't. So pick one and just try it out. When you are done, press the Done button to return to the main page. Remember: you are not compelled to do the tasks. We are just here to help you if you need it :)",
  "letsBegin": "Let's Begin",
  "playDateIntroTitle": "Welcome to Play Time",
  "playDateIntroBody": "So far on this journey, you've practiced starting conversations and building connections with other people.\n\nConversations are powerful because they help us learn about another person's experiences, thoughts, and story. However, conversations alone don't create shared memories.\n\nMany friendships grow stronger through shared experiences: playing games, working on activities together, exploring new places, or simply spending time together.\n\nIn this stage, you'll receive suggestions for games and activities that can be done with other people.\n\nThese are only recommendations. You are never required to play a particular game, and you're always free to choose a different activity that feels more comfortable or enjoyable.\n\nThe goal isn't to complete a specific game, but rather to make jokes, tease each other and make memories.",
  "hyggeIntroTitle": "Welcome to Hygge",
  "hyggeIntroBody": "For several years, Scandinavian countries have topped the list of the happiest countries in the world. There can be speculation, but we believe the culture of Hygge (pronounced hoo-gah) is one reason.\nHygge is a Danish concept that, in short, means taking time out of your busy schedule for coziness and comfort with friends. It doesn't involve specific things to do, but rather simple routines like drinking hot chocolate and playing board games under warm lighting.\n\nIn Hygge, the setting of comfort and coziness is more important than the activity itself. We are going to give you some suggestions, but we also advise you to make your own routines and activities.\n\nP.S. This is the ultimate form of social connection.",
  "bigTalkIntroTitle": "Welcome to Big Talk",
  "bigTalkIntroBody": "Almost everyone knows about small talk. However, it leads to shallow connections. The deeper we go into a person's life, the more connected we feel with them. On this idea, an undergraduate (Kalina Silverman) came up with a solution to loneliness and named it along the lines of small talk: Big Talk. In the following section, you will be given prompts that go deeper than basic questions. We advise you to practice these conversations with someone you already know (to make you feel comfortable with Big Talk), and later try them with strangers. Another piece of advice is to keep the conversation going for at least 5 minutes, because it would be awkward for the other person (and for your brain) if you ask a deep question and end the conversation abruptly. Your brain will likely crash from deep connection to suddenly zero.\nTo keep the conversation going you can remember small talk topics or just ask other related things to the question you just asked. As always, if you feel you aren't able to do it at any point, you can opt out, but remember short-term anxiety and doubt won't solve your long-term loneliness.",
  "congratulations": "Congratulations 🎉",
  "graduationBody": "This marks the end of your journey through the guided stages. You started from small conversations and made it to the ultimate medium of social connection: Hygge. If your goal of connecting with others or feeling less lonely was not met, you can write us an email and we can help you directly. However, if you feel less lonely than before (it's fine if it isn't zero), we advise you to keep going down this road of maintaining and building connections. We suggest this because if you stop, your social connections will shrink, and a brain used to your current level of connection will feel socially hungry (aka lonely).\n\nWe do not ask you to keep using this app, but we can still help you if you need ideas. After this point, the Let's connect button on your main screen will open a page that gives you a button for each stage you went through. If you need it, you can choose a stage and take prompts from there.\n\nInstead of saying goodbye, we are going to ask you a self-reflection question. Do you feel less lonely after taking our help?",
  "yesSmile": "Yes :)",
  "noSad": "No :(",

  "realityCheck1": "People usually enjoy conversations with strangers more than they expect.",
  "realityCheck2": "People expect only 40% of strangers to respond in a friendly manner to a spontaneous conversation, but in fact 92% do.",
  "realityCheck3": "At first meeting, people like you more than you think.",
  "realityCheck4": "The anticipation of a conversation is often scarier than the conversation itself.",
  "realityCheck5": "A brief conversation can improve both people's moods.",
  "realityCheck6": "You never know what new way of living you will discover today. You might even find a full-time philosopher!",
  "realityCheck7": "Deep connections start with talking, and talking starts with approaching someone on a random day.",
  "realityCheck8": "A connection doesn't have to be perfect the first time you meet. Two people who absolutely hate each other have been known to become best friends.",
  "realityCheck9": "Many people are hoping someone talks to them first.",
  "realityCheck10": "When we are hungry and have food in front of us, we eat. Loneliness is social hunger. When you find a person in front of you, take a bite of the social pie (don't worry, I'm not asking you to eat anyone).",
  "realityCheck11": "Small talk isn't the opposite of connection. It's often the doorway to it.",
  "realityCheck12": "Most people respond kindly to simple, respectful questions.",

  "bigTalkInsight1": "Meaningful friendships rarely grow from small talk alone.",
  "bigTalkInsight2": "Most people enjoy being asked thoughtful questions more than we expect.",
  "bigTalkInsight3": "Many people wish conversations would go deeper but are waiting for someone else to start.",
  "bigTalkInsight4": "Vulnerability often strengthens relationships rather than weakening them.",
  "bigTalkInsight5": "Being interested is more important than being interesting.",
  "bigTalkInsight6": "People usually remember how a conversation felt, not whether every word was perfect.",
  "bigTalkInsight7": "A meaningful question can create more connection than an hour of casual conversation.",
  "bigTalkInsight8": "Most people appreciate feeling genuinely listened to.",
  "bigTalkInsight9": "The goal of Big Talk is understanding, not impressing.",
  "bigTalkInsight10": "Connection grows when people share experiences, values, and perspectives.",

